# Descriptor units and the typed computation boundary · `spec:nrz:descriptor-units-boundary`

This decision connects original-expression checking, logical matrix shapes and descriptor storage (`req:vision:expression-and-symbol-contract`), (`spec:nrz:matrix-shapes`), (`spec:nrz:descriptor-heap-computation`). The decision prototype is `prototypes/descriptors`, crate `nrz-descriptor-prototype`; it is an independently built workspace member with no production dependents. Its two dependencies are `nrz-units` and `nrz-algebra`.

## Representation · `sec:nrz:descriptor-unit-representation`

**Decision (Descriptor unit authority)** · `dec:nrz:descriptor-unit-authority`

Units are descriptor data. The authoritative quantity object is the existing `nrz-units::Descriptor`, carrying exact rational SI exponents, semantic kind, exact measure, affine role and any count domain. The CAS checks original expressions and admitted operations at plan construction, binding and publication. Rust types carry handle category, precision, evidence-owning validation state and roles fixed by engine code. The engine does not duplicate dimension algebra in a static quantity layer. Normalized kernels receive plain scalar coordinates only through a validated boundary (`def:nrz:checked-descriptor-boundary`).

Rational exponent reduction remains the algebra core's responsibility: `2/4` and `1/2` are the same exact exponent. Rational and integral-only admission are explicit registry policies; a registered root's dimension and kind still pass CAS checks. An integer-only comparison type explicitly refuses fractional results. Counts retain exact whole-number domains and literal checks. The prototype refuses every count-valued floating arena entry, even a representable small integer; production discrete storage and arithmetic remain foundations work. Dimensions do not identify kinds: counts, ratios and angles remain distinct at zero dimension, and affine points differ from differences (`dec:vision:data-time-unit-descriptors`).

**Definition (Quantity schema key)** · `def:nrz:quantity-schema-key`

A quantity key wraps a CAS descriptor and delegates equality to its exact `Eq`, including measure names, scales, origins, roles, kinds and count domains. Convertible descriptors need not be equal: equal-scale aliases with different measure names remain distinct. Conversion compatibility is a checked relation, not interning equality. The hash uses public dimension, measure name, scale, offset and role fields; omitting kind and private count bounds is lawful because exact equality resolves collisions. Tests deliberately collide angle/ratio and distinct count domains. No debug rendering or independent dimension normal form defines identity. Changes to CAS descriptor equality require a separate units change.

Quantity and shape IDs include an interner scope; equal local indices from independent pools do not compare equal. Schemas are immutable after admission. Registry/context snapshots are separate from descriptor equality, since a rule environment changes which operations are valid without changing a quantity's meaning. The prototype owns its snapshots and assigns process-local identities; it defines no persistent cache-key or serialization format.

A logical shape key includes ordered codomain rows and domain columns with coordinate meanings, extents and each entry's quantity-schema ID. General shapes also require partition, entry-map, structure and evidence-scope contracts; the prototype exposes only full dense shapes with identity entry maps. Logical vectorization is `i + m*j`, independent of physical layout. Value identity, source identity, precision, arena offsets and batch ordinals are excluded. Coordinate maps are separate checked contracts, including typed positive physical references and affine origins; equal shape IDs cannot authorize a changed map (`def:nrz:matrix-shape`), (`def:nrz:shape-normalization`).

A companion has its own quantity and shape schemas and its own storage. It captures the target handle, value version and ordered logical entry selection; covariance captures both sides. A two-entry center, four-entry covariance and scalar norm need different shapes. Covariance dimensions and measures come from the CAS uncertainty constructor and paired scale products, never operator quotients. Shared schemas neither merge values nor establish common sources or independence (`def:nrz:companion-shape`), (`rule:nrz:batch-source-covariance`).

## Typed access · `sec:nrz:descriptor-typed-boundary`

**Definition (Checked descriptor boundary)** · `def:nrz:checked-descriptor-boundary`

`Handle<Category, Precision>` has private heap, slot, slot-generation and arena-generation fields. Categories are value, companion, factor resource and workspace. Precision is the sealed plain scalar type, f32 or f64. Stored handles contain no borrowed slices. A handle's type does not prove that its reference is still live: resolution checks identity, generations, category and range, while coefficient mutation advances a distinct value version. Counters refuse exhaustion instead of wrapping. The small prototype retires slots without reuse; production allocation and alias analysis remain matrix responsibilities (`def:nrz:descriptor-reference`), (`rule:nrz:descriptor-lifetime-and-mutation`).

Validation states belong to the objects retaining their evidence. `Plan<Unchecked>` owns original expressions, an immutable context snapshot, explicit symbol-to-slot associations, shape snapshots and map contracts. Checking original operations, scalar meanings, ordered shapes and supported recipe selection produces `Plan<Checked>` with retained CAS witnesses. Its proposed `Binding` captures concrete references and versions but has no kernel-view method. Binding validation checks actual schemas, versions, paired companion coverage, supported uncertainty and exact normalization maps. `Bound` then holds an exclusive heap borrow and alone lends `NormalizedView`; fixed point and difference lifts inspect dynamic descriptors before selecting their role marker. A `PhysicalView` cannot be supplied to the normalized kernel (`rule:nrz:plan-validation`), (`rule:nrz:binding-validation`).

The prototype's checked recipe is deliberately narrow: only its two original resistor equations select its toy kernel. A different, dimensionally valid expression is refused as unsupported. This prevents a generic scalar units check from being mistaken for proof that an arbitrary execution recipe implements an expression. CAS diagnostics retain paths, source spans, definition chains and expected/derived meanings inside typed errors with client node, slot and binding context. Runtime storage refusals distinguish schema, shape, reference, version, map, numerical and unsupported cases.

**Rule (Publication validation)** · `rule:nrz:descriptor-publication-validation`

The kernel returns a private unpublished candidate tagged with its binding identity. Publication borrows the candidate and consumes the binding, rechecks candidate origin and output shape contracts, checks output expressions against the retained CAS snapshot, restores through the same maps, and validates finite values and both covariance axes before appending either result. All fallible semantic checks precede one append of the prepared center and companion records. A candidate from another binding is refused even when shapes and maps agree. Retaining a candidate cannot authorize another publication because each new binding has a distinct identity. The exclusive borrow prevents input mutation during execution and publication. A published handle still requires fresh resolution after later mutation. Published means semantically admitted; numerical certification requires its own assurance and certificate evidence (`def:vision:operation-result-and-failure`).

## Prototype evidence · `sec:nrz:descriptor-prototype-evidence`

**Example (Resistor pipeline)** · `ex:nrz:descriptor-resistor-prototype`

The physical unknowns are `x = (v, i)` in `(V, A)`, where `i` is current through a resistor of exact resistance `R = 2 Ω`. A single shared random source `s` has center `4 V` and finite second moment with variance `1/4 V²`. The original equations are `v - R*i = zero_v` and `v + zero_r*i = s`. The corresponding physical operator is `[[1, -R], [1, zero_r]]`, with typed ratio coefficients and typed physical zeros; scalar checking and ordered shape checks both precede computation. This is smaller than the ladder example and has a different current convention, so its signs are derived independently (`ex:nrz:resistor-shape-covariance`).

With `S_X = (2 V, 1 A)` and `S_Y = (2 V, 2 V)`, the operator `S_Y⁻¹ A S_X` is `[[1, -1], [1, 0]]` and the normalized right-hand side is `(0, 2)`. The toy kernel applies a two-by-two inverse to obtain `(2, 2)`, restoring `(4 V, 2 A)`. The exact linear sensitivity to the source is `J = (1, 1/R)`. Thus `J Var(s) Jᵀ` is `[[1/4 V², 1/8 V A], [1/8 V A, 1/16 A²]]`. Paired solution-reference scaling gives `1/16` in every normalized covariance entry. The dyadic coefficients permit bit-exact expectations in both f32 and f64; a separate rational derivation checks the equations and covariance products. This statistical law is neither a deterministic radius nor a floating-error certificate.

Schema tests exercise exact equality, coarse-hash collisions, rational exponent policy, distinct interner scopes, ordered axes, distinct center/covariance/norm extents, count refusal and independent value identities. Boundary tests reject wrong typed zeros, invalid original children hidden by zero or cancellation, swapped axes, foreign or stale handles, changed value versions, stale or wrongly targeted companions, changed maps, unsupported deterministic-radius coverage, wrong output contracts, foreign candidates and nonfinite data. Separate rational tests cover centimetres/metres, Celsius/kelvin point and difference conversions, explicit affine origins and paired covariance scales. General floating conversions are not claimed exact.

Every rustdoc `compile_fail` example has a positive twin. The cases reject binding an unchecked plan, obtaining a view from an unvalidated binding, substituting each non-value category, mixing precision, using physical coordinates at normalized kernel entry, substituting a point for a difference, forging checked evidence, treating an unpublished candidate as published, and mutating the heap while a bound view remains live. The static comparison adds unequal-exponent addition. Successful compilation, linting and these tests belong to the gate of record at every commit (`rule:vision:gate-of-record`). No compiler diagnostic text, diagnostic code or measured compilation wall is inferred from a source example.

**Caveat (Prototype scope)** · `cav:nrz:descriptor-prototype-scope`

The package is a decision experiment, not an engine API or production heap, planner, allocator, factorization or certification kernel. It admits one contiguous dense resistor recipe, identity entry maps, coherent input measures, explicit fixed normalization contracts and one finite-second-moment shared-source model. Affine map construction is tested separately; the resistor solve uses differences. General compatible input measure conversion at binding, conversion enclosures, discrete arenas, structured and strided views, arbitrary expression lowering, factor reuse, nonlinear uncertainty, multiple shared sources and batches require later work. No positive-semidefiniteness or physical-model validation follows from covariance descriptor admission. Broader shape and computation evidence requirements remain in their implementing bites (`req:nrz:shape-implementation-evidence`), (`req:nrz:computation-implementation-evidence`).

## Static comparison · `sec:nrz:descriptor-static-comparison`

**Table (Static alternatives)** · `tab:nrz:descriptor-static-alternatives`

|Alternative|Soundness|Expressiveness|Inference|Diagnostics|Compilation cost|Lint compatibility|
|-----------|---------|--------------|---------|-----------|----------------|------------------|
|Descriptor authority with access-state and role markers|CAS meaning plus private evidence owners, version checks and normalized access|Rational exponents, registered kinds, affine roles and heterogeneous schemas arrive as data|No static exponent annotations at kernel entry|Typed boundary refusals and preserved original-expression diagnostics|One normalized kernel per precision; runtime checking and interning costs require measurement|Safe Rust and existing pedantic policy; gate remains authoritative|
|Seven const-generic integer exponents|Checked lift and same-type addition; CAS still checks semantic meaning|Integral subset in the compiled probe; fractional descriptors explicitly refused|Products, quotients and roots use explicit checked result witnesses|Unequal-exponent addition has a rejection example and positive twin; no diagnostic text is presumed|Additional physical type instantiations and annotations; no isolated timing claim|Compiled in the same workspace and posture, without exponent algebra duplication|
|Type-level integers or rationals|Can encode more composition if normalized laws and CAS agreement are maintained|Potentially richer static algebra, but data-provided kinds, roles and shape identities still need checking|Trait relations can infer more results, with coherence and annotation tradeoffs|Requires a separate diagnostic evaluation before making quality claims|More trait solving and type normalization are plausible costs, not measured results|No impossibility claim; no second arithmetic implementation or dependency is introduced for this decision|
|Closed generated dimension table|Can make admitted finite operations explicit|A finite vocabulary cannot cover the full rational and data-registered contract without fallbacks|Convenient inference inside its chosen closed world|Can provide specific errors for enumerated operations|Generated surface and regeneration costs depend on table size; unmeasured|Potentially compatible, but generation and completeness obligations add another maintained boundary|

The compiled `static_probe` uses seven integral const parameters and a checked descriptor lift. Addition requires the same exponent type, then invokes CAS addition for kind, role and count closure. Products, quotients and square roots invoke CAS operations and compare an explicit result witness; the prototype does not infer result exponents statically. Shared cases include valid resistor products/quotients, standard-uncertainty roots, incompatible kinds at equal exponents, point addition, bounded count closure, measure conversion and refusal of rational lifts. The same descriptor-bound normalized kernel is reused (`ex:nrz:descriptor-resistor-prototype`).

The chosen boundary follows the criteria in order: soundness needs retained original evidence and validated maps; expressiveness needs data-time rational and semantic contracts; inference gains from physical exponent types do not survive dimensionless kernel entry; diagnostics need original source locations and client context; compilation-cost claims need measured comparisons; lint compatibility is established by the actual workspace gate (`req:vision:units-design-criteria`). A normalized zero-exponent type alone does not establish map provenance. The three full static representation alternatives are therefore rejected as engine authority, while narrow fixed role and access-state markers are retained. A future measured benefit in a fixed physical subdomain may justify an optional adapter without replacing descriptor authority.

## Implementation ownership · `sec:nrz:descriptor-implementation-path`

**Decision (Prototype promotion)** · `dec:nrz:descriptor-prototype-promotion`

Promote the contracts and their positive/negative cases through the existing package boundaries. Units owns descriptor equality, exact dimension and semantic rules, original-expression evidence and reusable publication witnesses. Matrix owns schema storage, category/precision handles, generations, checked layouts, normalized views, atomic result/companion publication and numerical assurance. Physics owns immutable checked plans, symbol-to-slot lowering, binding policies, batches and source relationships over matrix's public boundary. Algebra remains physics-free. Engine packages must not depend on the prototype (`rule:vision:layer-enforcement`).

Rewrite the append-only toy heap, fixed two-equation recipe, copied two-by-two kernel and ad hoc process-local identity scheme when the production owners implement their contracts. Carry over the invariant tests and dyadic rational oracle. Keep the const-generic probe only as comparison evidence or delete it once the decision no longer needs executable comparison; it is not a production quantity API. Production conversion bounds, count storage, factor domains, full shape support and certified numerical results remain foundations and matrix obligations. Source documentation notices identify the publication-witness and normalized-kernel boundaries without changing existing package behavior.

The versioned problem-data schema, generator checks and migration are implemented together by the separate schema lane (`spec:nrz:problem-data-schema`), under (`req:vision:unit-data-migration`). Units/docgen owns that implementation and NRZ coordinates its contract. Foundations retains numerical quantities, conversion enclosures, numerical companions and matrix contracts. The prototype decision remains limited to representation; the schema specification and implementing migration now own problem declarations and generator behavior. Existing numerical behavior, measured feasibility facts and bite numbering remain unchanged.
